WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.905 --> 00:00:07.694
A worker never writes to your checkout.

2
00:00:07.694 --> 00:00:13.128
It works in a git worktree of its own, and when it sends its done, what that

3
00:00:13.128 --> 00:00:16.918
worktree holds shows up as a change, waiting for you.

4
00:00:16.918 --> 00:00:22.781
Let us speed the simulation up until a few workers are done, then pause it, so the

5
00:00:22.781 --> 00:00:23.997
list holds still.

6
00:00:29.085 --> 00:00:32.091
Capital W opens the next change.

7
00:00:32.091 --> 00:00:39.794
A change of several files lists them above its patch, with the lines each adds and

8
00:00:39.794 --> 00:00:47.497
removes, and when they are of more than one kind, the lines by kind first: source,

9
00:00:47.497 --> 00:00:49.094
tests, generated.

10
00:00:49.094 --> 00:00:56.703
j and k scroll; the brackets jump from file to file; w wraps the long lines under

11
00:00:56.703 --> 00:00:57.736
themselves.

12
00:00:58.937 --> 00:01:03.273
c puts a note on the line at the top of the view.

13
00:01:03.273 --> 00:01:10.617
Scroll a line of a hunk to the top, press c, write, and enter keeps it; the line is

14
00:01:10.617 --> 00:01:11.237
marked.

15
00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:13.714
A second note, further down.

16
00:01:13.714 --> 00:01:19.908
The notes belong to this patch: they stay with it until you send them.

17
00:01:29.307 --> 00:01:35.438
n sends the notes to the worker in one message, each under its file and line with

18
00:01:35.438 --> 00:01:41.494
the line itself quoted, and the change goes back like a reject: not applied, the

19
00:01:41.494 --> 00:01:43.841
worker goes on with your notes.

20
00:01:43.841 --> 00:01:47.398
It is the third answer beside apply and reject.

21
00:01:48.599 --> 00:01:49.877
The next change.

22
00:01:49.877 --> 00:01:54.908
x chooses the hunk at the top; a check mark goes on its header.

23
00:01:54.908 --> 00:02:01.537
a then applies only the chosen hunks, staged in your checkout, and the rest is read

24
00:02:01.537 --> 00:02:06.090
again from the worktree and waits as a change of its own.

25
00:02:15.721 --> 00:02:16.830
Another change.

26
00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:22.892
The greater-than key opens a message to its worker with the hunk at the top quoted

27
00:02:22.892 --> 00:02:26.957
under your line, in a diff fence with its file's names.

28
00:02:26.957 --> 00:02:29.693
Write the question above it and send.

29
00:02:39.997 --> 00:02:47.108
And a change you do not want: r rejects it and opens the composer addressed to its

30
00:02:47.108 --> 00:02:49.103
worker, so you say why.

31
00:02:49.103 --> 00:02:55.868
The worker reads your reason and tries another way; nothing of it touched your

32
00:02:55.868 --> 00:02:56.648
checkout.

33
00:03:01.626 --> 00:03:04.021
Let the simulation run again.

34
00:03:04.021 --> 00:03:08.151
Changes are not the only thing that waits for you.

35
00:03:08.151 --> 00:03:14.347
A flag is a command or an edit a vendor would have asked you about: routine

36
00:03:14.347 --> 00:03:20.790
commands, the build and the tests, run on their own; the rest wait here, shown

37
00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:23.846
whole, for a to allow or r to refuse.

38
00:03:23.846 --> 00:03:27.894
A question mark is a question an agent asked you.

39
00:03:27.894 --> 00:03:32.437
Capital S puts the item at hand off and opens the next.

40
00:03:36.771 --> 00:03:41.662
That is the gate: nothing reaches your checkout without your yes.

